10:46:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your teacher was repeating a very common older explanation, and it has a grain of truth that is easy to overstate. Glass is **amorphous**: its atoms lack the regular crystal lattice of quartz or salt. Because of that, it can undergo extremely slow structural relaxation under some conditions. But that does **not** mean ordinary window glass flows under gravity like a liquid on historical timescales. At room temperature, soda-lime window glass has an enormous viscosity—roughly \(10^{18}\) to \(10^{20+}\) pascal-seconds, depending on composition and temperature. Any sagging of a cathedral pane from its own weight would be immeasurably small over centuries. In practice, glass must be heated close to its glass-transition range before it noticeably softens and flows. The thicker-at-the-bottom cathedral-window observation is real for many panes, but it is not evidence of flow. Medieval glassmaking methods—especially crown-glass and cylinder-glass processes—produced uneven sheets. Glaziers often installed panes with the thicker side down, though not always; some old panes are thicker at the top or side. So the precise version is: - Glass is an **amorphous solid**, not a conventional liquid. - It may exhibit extremely slow molecular rearrangement or creep in certain conditions. - At cathedral-window temperatures, it does not flow enough to explain their uneven thickness. - The unevenness was overwhelmingly present when the panes were made and installed.
