10:46:22 | INFO | 🤖 Target: The pitch-drop analogy is useful for showing that something can look solid while still being a very viscous liquid—but glass and pitch are not equally viscous. Pitch at room temperature has a viscosity on the order of roughly \(10^8\)–\(10^{10}\) Pa·s (varying hugely with composition and temperature). That is high enough that a drop can take years or decades, but low enough that gravity can still deform it visibly. Typical window glass at room temperature has a viscosity more like \(10^{18}\)–\(10^{20}\) Pa·s or higher: around **billions to trillions of times more viscous than pitch**. That difference is decisive. A rough scaling is: \[ \text{flow rate} \propto \frac{1}{\text{viscosity}}. \] So if a pitch drop changes visibly over 10 years, a comparably stressed piece of glass could require on the order of \(10^{11}\)–\(10^{13}\) years—or more—to show a comparable change. The universe is only about \(1.4\times10^{10}\) years old. Also, pitch is often described as a liquid because it has a measurable ability to flow at room temperature. Window glass below its glass-transition temperature is conventionally classified as a solid: it has elastic rigidity and, for ordinary engineering purposes, no observable gravitational flow on human or archaeological timescales. So: pitch demonstrates “very viscous materials can fool your intuition.” It does not demonstrate that all amorphous solids, including cold glass, flow appreciably.
