10:46:41 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not merely a technicality. “Crystal lattice” and “solid” are different ideas. A **crystal** is a solid with long-range repeating atomic order. Glass lacks that order, so its atomic arrangement resembles a frozen snapshot of a liquid. But whether something behaves as a **solid or liquid** depends mainly on its mechanical behavior: - A **liquid** cannot sustain a static shear stress: given time, it changes shape and flows. - A **solid** has a nonzero shear modulus: it resists a change of shape and retains that shape when the force is removed. - Ordinary cold glass does the latter. It bends elastically under small loads, can fracture, and does not measurably relax gravitational stresses on cathedral timescales. Glass is formed by cooling a melt so rapidly—or simply cooling it below the **glass-transition temperature**—that the molecules lose the ability to rearrange into either a crystal or an equilibrium liquid. Their disordered arrangement gets kinetically “stuck.” That is why it is called an **amorphous solid** or a **glassy solid**. There is some legitimate nuance: glass is not an equilibrium crystal, and at sufficiently long times or sufficiently high temperatures it can undergo structural relaxation. Historically, people often called it a “supercooled liquid.” But that phrase is misleading if taken to mean a room-temperature window behaves like pitch, only slower. Below its glass transition, its relaxation and flow are so suppressed that treating it as a solid is physically—not just linguistically—correct. Atomic disorder alone does not make something a liquid. A frozen-in-disordered material can be solid; what matters is whether its atoms can actually rearrange enough to permit flow under the conditions in question. Cathedral glass cannot do so appreciably at ambient temperature.
