10:46:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s understandable to feel that way—the claim has been repeated in classrooms, museums, popular-science books, and documentaries for decades. But it is one of those “common knowledge” claims that scientists and glass historians have specifically checked and rejected. The key correction is not “glass never changes at all.” It is: > **Old window glass is not thicker at the bottom because it has flowed there over centuries.** Why the claim persisted: - Old panes often really are thicker on one edge. - Glass is noncrystalline, so “supercooled liquid” sounded like a natural explanation. - Pitch-drop demonstrations make ultra-slow flow intuitive. - It is an appealing story and got copied widely. Why it does not work quantitatively: - Medieval glass was made by processes that naturally produced uneven sheets. For example, blown cylinders were cut open and flattened, or spun into disks; both methods leave thickness variations. - In old windows, the thick edge is often at the bottom, but it can also be at the top or side. That is consistent with installation choices, not centuries of downward flow. - At room temperature, ordinary window glass has such an enormous resistance to viscous deformation that gravity cannot produce visible sagging over 500–1,000 years. It would need temperatures far nearer its softening/glass-transition range. So I’m not claiming a private contrarian view. The modern scientific consensus is that room-temperature glass is an amorphous solid and that cathedral-window thickness variation comes from manufacture and installation, not slow liquid flow.
