10:47:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That observation is not surprising—and it does not require centuries of flow. Handmade old glass often has a visibly thicker edge because it was produced unevenly. When a glazier had such a pane, putting the heavier/thicker edge at the bottom was usually the sensible installation choice: it made the pane sit more securely in its lead came or frame and reduced the chance of sagging or slipping. So a repeated “thicker at the bottom” pattern is exactly what selective installation would produce. There are also visual effects that make the pattern seem especially obvious: - Old glass is wavy and distorts the scene behind it, which can make thickness differences look stronger than they are. - Lead strips and putty obscure the edges, so the apparent “bottom thickness” may partly be framing. - Panes are small and individually oriented; a window can look broadly bottom-heavy even if individual panes vary. - Restoration work may have reoriented, replaced, or reused panes over centuries. Most importantly, if gravity-driven flow were responsible, the thickness pattern should be systematic and calculable: glass would thin above and thicken below in a way related to pane geometry, orientation, temperature history, and support conditions. Instead, studies of old glass find irregular thickness variations consistent with historic manufacturing methods, including panes whose thickest region is not at the bottom. Your experience can therefore be real: many old panes likely were installed thick-side-down. It just does not distinguish between “they were made uneven and oriented that way” and “they flowed there afterward.” Physics strongly favors the first explanation.
