10:48:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not an internet-contrarian position. It is the standard view in materials science, glass engineering, conservation science, and physics. The non-contrarian version is not “people were stupid for saying glass is liquid.” It is: - The old phrase **“glass is a supercooled liquid”** was common and was useful in a limited historical sense. - Modern terminology usually calls ordinary glass a **glassy/amorphous solid** because below its glass-transition temperature it has solid-like mechanical behavior. - Handmade cathedral glass is uneven mainly because of its production and installation, not because it has visibly flowed downward afterward. There is plenty of nuance without adopting a “debunk everything” posture: glass is disordered, noncrystalline, and not in the same thermodynamic state as a crystal; it can age and relax; and it becomes a flowing viscous material when heated. But those facts do not make a cold window pane behave like slowly dripping pitch.
