10:49:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not need to conclude that everything you learned was unreliable. A better lesson is that science education often gives simplified versions first, and later evidence or better measurements refine them. The cathedral-glass claim is a good example because it was not a core law of science; it was a memorable anecdote attached to real ideas about glass. The core ideas remain sound: - Glass is noncrystalline and has a disordered atomic structure. - It is made by cooling a melt into a glassy state. - Heating it enough makes it soften and flow. - At normal temperatures, it behaves mechanically as a solid. - Handmade historic panes were often uneven from the start. Science is trustworthy not because every classroom slogan is permanent, but because claims can be checked against measurements, manufacturing history, and physical predictions—and revised when they fail. A useful habit is to treat catchy “everyone knows” facts as worth verifying, especially when they are simple stories about complex phenomena. That is not a reason for cynicism; it is the process by which science becomes more reliable.
