10:49:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. A material’s origin does not permanently determine its state. Water can start as a liquid and become ice; molten metal can become either a crystal or an amorphous metal; melted sugar can become a glassy candy. What matters is its present structure and, especially, how it responds to forces at its current temperature. When molten glass is cooled below its **glass-transition temperature**, its disordered arrangement becomes kinetically trapped. It does not crystallize, but it also no longer has the molecular mobility needed to behave as a liquid. It becomes an **amorphous solid**: disordered like a liquid in structure, rigid like a solid in mechanics. So “not crystalline” does not mean “liquid.” There is a real category in between: a glassy solid.
