10:49:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not as a blanket rule. “Amorphous” means **lacking long-range crystal order**; it does not specify a single flow rate or even guarantee appreciable flow under a given condition. Different amorphous materials can behave very differently: - **Pitch / tar:** amorphous and capable of visible room-temperature flow. - **Rubber:** amorphous polymeric material; deforms and relaxes strongly. - **Window glass:** amorphous, but its atomic rearrangements are extremely suppressed at room temperature. - **Amorphous silicon or metallic glass:** amorphous solids that are rigid and may fracture rather than flow. Temperature relative to the material’s glass-transition temperature, chemical composition, stress, and timescale matter. A window pane well below its glass-transition range is in a rigid glassy state. A pitch sample at room temperature may be much closer to—or effectively above—its relevant transition and can therefore creep perceptibly. Thus the category-level fact is: amorphous materials can have time-dependent relaxation and may flow under suitable conditions. It does not entail that every amorphous object, including a cold cathedral window, flows enough for gravity to reshape it over centuries.
