10:50:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It would be suggestive **if age were the only relevant difference**—but it is not. Older windows are much more likely to be: - handmade by crown- or cylinder-glass methods, which produced uneven sheets; - cut into small panes with a noticeably thicker edge; - installed by glaziers who often favored putting that thicker edge down; - preserved or restored in ways that retain those historical features. Newer windows are commonly made by much more uniform industrial processes, especially float glass, so they start out with nearly uniform thickness. Thus “older glass is more often bottom-heavy” can arise because **age is correlated with manufacturing method and installation practice**, not because the material has had longer to flow. To establish flow, one would need to compare otherwise similar panes—same original thickness distribution, orientation, composition, support, and temperature history—and show a predictable increase in bottom-thickening with time. That pattern is not what the evidence shows. Old panes can have their thickest part at the top or side, and their irregularity matches known production methods. And the physics matters as an independent check: at normal window temperatures, soda-lime glass is far too resistant to viscous deformation for gravity to create the observed millimeter-scale thickness differences in centuries. So the age correlation is real-looking and understandable, but it is a classic confounding effect: older panes were made and installed differently from newer ones.
