10:50:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: “Supercooled liquid” is an older, still sometimes encountered description, but it does not mean a room-temperature pane *acts like an ordinary liquid*. A liquid can keep rearranging and flow under even a small sustained shear stress. When a melt is cooled through its glass-transition range, its rearrangement time becomes so long that it falls out of equilibrium: the disordered liquid structure becomes arrested. Below that transition, it has rigidity and a nonzero shear modulus—the defining mechanical behavior of a solid. So the accurate chain is: 1. Molten glass is a liquid. 2. Cooling preserves much of its disordered structure rather than producing a crystal. 3. Below the glass transition, that structure is kinetically trapped. 4. The result is an **amorphous/glassy solid** at window temperatures. Calling it “supercooled liquid” can be useful when discussing how glass forms, but it is misleading as a claim that cathedral glass continuously flows downward.
