10:51:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Then the literal answer is: **at ordinary temperatures, window glass is not a liquid.** It is an amorphous, noncrystalline solid. Its atomic arrangement is disordered and resembles that of a liquid, which is why the older phrase “supercooled liquid” became popular. But a material’s state is not decided by whether it has a crystal lattice. Cold glass has rigidity: it supports shear stress, keeps its shape, and does not undergo appreciable gravity-driven flow on century-scale times. Glass is literally a viscous liquid when sufficiently hot. At room temperature it is a glassy solid whose structure is kinetically arrested. It can undergo tiny relaxation and chemical aging, but that is not the same as a window slowly draining downward. So the literal claim “cathedral panes are slowly flowing like an ultra-viscous liquid” is not supported by the material physics or the evidence from historic glass.
